1. Service Mesh:
    - A Service Mesh in Kubernetes is an infrastructure layer that manages service-to-service communication inside a microservices cluster. It handles networking concerns like:
        Traffic routing
        Load balancing
        Service discovery
        Mutual TLS (mTLS)
        Observability
        Retries & circuit breaking
        Security policies
    - The two most popular Kubernetes service meshes are:
        Istio
        Linkerd

    - How a Service Mesh Works
        A service mesh usually injects a sidecar proxy beside each application pod.
    - Example:
        [App Container] <-> [Proxy Sidecar]
    All traffic goes through the proxy, allowing centralized control.
    Common proxy:
        Envoy (used by Istio)
        Linkerd2-proxy (used by Linkerd)

2. Istio:
    - Key Features
        Advanced traffic management
        Canary deployments
        A/B testing
        Fault injection
        mTLS encryption
        Rich observability
        Policy enforcement
        Multi-cluster support

    - Architecture
    Main components:
        Envoy sidecars
        Istiod (control plane)
        Ingress/Egress gateways
    - Example Use Cases
        Blue/Green deployments
        API security
        Complex routing rules
        Enterprise microservices platforms
    - Pros
        Extremely powerful
        Feature rich
        Enterprise-grade
        Large ecosystem
    - Cons
        More complex
        Higher resource usage
        Steeper learning curve

3. Linkerd:
    - Key Features
        Lightweight
        Simple installation
        Automatic mTLS
        Fast performance
        Kubernetes-native
        Easy observability
    - Architecture
    Components:
        Linkerd control plane
        Rust-based lightweight proxy
        CLI + dashboard
    - Example Use Cases
        Small to medium clusters
        Simpler microservice environments
        Teams wanting easy adoption
    - Pros
        Easier to operate
        Lower latency
        Lower memory footprint
        Beginner friendly
    - Cons
        Fewer advanced traffic features
        Less flexible than Istio

4. Istio vs Linkerd
    Feature	            Istio	                    Linkerd
    Complexity	        High	                    Low
    Resource Usage	    Higher	                    Lower
    Performance	        Good	                    Excellent
    Traffic Control	    Advanced	                Basic-Moderate
    Learning Curve	    Steep	                    Easy
    Best For	        Large enterprise setups	    Simpler Kubernetes apps
    Proxy	            Envoy	                    linkerd2-proxy
    mTLS	            Yes	                        Yes

5. Example Installation
    Install Istio
        curl -L https://istio.io/downloadIstio | sh -
        istioctl install --set profile=demo -y
        kubectl label namespace default istio-injection=enabled
    Install Linkerd
        curl -sL https://run.linkerd.io/install | sh
        linkerd install | kubectl apply -f -
        linkerd check

6. When to Choose Which?
    Choose Istio if:
        You need advanced traffic management
        You run large enterprise clusters
        You need fine-grained policies
        You want extensive telemetry
    Choose Linkerd if:
        You want simplicity
        You need lightweight infrastructure
        You are starting with service meshes
        You prioritize performance and low overhead

7. Simple Analogy
    Without service mesh:
        Service A ---> Service B
    With service mesh:
        Service A -> Proxy -> Proxy -> Service B
    The proxies manage:
        encryption
        retries
        metrics
        routing
        authentication